<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1822 CE · capital Rio de Janeiro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A transatlantic monarchy ruled from Rio de Janeiro, created when Brazil was raised to a kingdom equal to Portugal in 1815.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> invasion, the Portuguese court sailed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> in 1807-1808 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>. On 16 December 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-of-portugal">Prince Regent John</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from colony to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brazil">Kingdom of Brazil</a>, uniting it with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-algarve">Kingdom of the Algarve</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio</a> became the seat of government of a global empire, with new ministries, a royal library, a bank and opened ports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-of-portugal">John VI</a>, acclaimed king in 1818 after the death of Maria I, stayed in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>, while Portugal was governed by a regency under strong British influence and resented its subordinate status.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> in 1820 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-of-portugal">John VI</a> to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1821, leaving his son Pedro as regent. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-constituent-cortes">Lisbon Cortes</a> tried to reduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> status, Pedro declared independence on 7 September 1822, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves.jpg" alt="United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: 117-M-I-K-E · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_of_Portugal,_Brazil,_and_the_Algarves_(1815-1825).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rio de Janeiro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maria I, John VI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>, <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United Kingdom of Portugal and Brazil, Reino Unido de Portugal, Brasil e Algarves</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves" aria-label="United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3525 3023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3845 3279L3846 3279M3845 3279L3845 3279M3845 3279L3575 3696M3845 3279L4036 2734M3845 3279L4773 1045M3845 3279L4773 1044"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3846 3279h0M3845 3279h0M3575 3696h0M4036 2734h0M4773 1045h0M4773 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3845 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3845 3279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rio de Janeiro. <a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
